YOUR TASK: research the DEMAND side — what content creators (YouTubers long-form, short-form TikTok/Reels/Shorts creators, video podcasters, educators/course makers, streamers repurposing VODs) actually struggle with in editing as of 2025-2026, and what makes them switch editors. Use creator-economy surveys and reports (e.g. YouTube/TikTok creator reports, Adobe/Linktree/ConvertKit/Kajabi creator surveys), r/NewTubers, r/VideoEditing, r/podcasting, r/finalcutpro, r/davinciresolve, r/premiere, YouTube creator forums, and well-known creator-educator videos/articles; quantify where possible (time spent editing, share outsourcing editing, top requested features). Capture: per segment, the workflow and the top pains; switching triggers; complaints about CapCut (e.g. terms of service, US availability, paywalls), Premiere (subscription, stability), Resolve (learning curve), Final Cut (stagnation), Descript (performance) etc.; platform requirements in 2026 (Shorts length limits, safe zones, HDR upload, captions, chapters, aspect ratios, loudness); and creators' attitudes to AI in editing (welcome vs. distrust; privacy/on-device; cost). ${WEB}`,
  },
]

const researchOut = await parallel([
  () => agent(inventoryPrompt, { label: 'inventory:frame-job', phase: 'Research', schema: INVENTORY_SCHEMA }),
  ...segments.map(s => () => agent(s.prompt, { label: `research:${s.key}`, phase: 'Research', schema: s.key === 'demand' ? DEMAND_SCHEMA : RESEARCH_SCHEMA })),
])

const inventory = researchOut[0]
const research = {}
segments.forEach((s, i) => { research[s.key] = researchOut[i + 1] })
const missing = ['inventory', ...segments.map(s => s.key)].filter((k, i) => !researchOut[i])
if (missing.length) log(`research incomplete: ${missing.join(', ')} returned nothing`)
if (!inventory) return { error: 'inventory failed', research }

const evidence = JSON.stringify({ inventory, research })

// ---------- Phase 2: strategy panel ----------
phase('Strategy')

const ANGLES = [
  {
    key: 'creator-jobs',
    brief: `ANGLE: CREATOR JOBS FIRST. Walk each creator workflow (long-form talking head, short-form vertical and long→short repurposing, video podcast, screen tutorial/demo, b-roll vlog, stream highlights). For each, find the step where a creator using Frame Job today would give up and open CapCut/Descript/Premiere instead. Propose the bets that remove those give-up points for the most creators, ranked by how many creators and how often.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'differentiation',
    brief: `ANGLE: DIFFERENTIATION AND MOAT. Catching up feature-for-feature with CapCut or Premiere does not make a single-developer open-source Mac editor "leading". Find where Frame Job can be BEST-IN-CLASS or category-defining given what it uniquely has — the whole editor as MCP tools and an in-app agent, open source, bring-your-own-key AI, on-device Apple ML, a precise audio chain, a programmable graphics engine, Mac-native performance and HDR. Think: agent-native editing (an agent that makes the rough cut, the shorts, the captions, the chapters from a brief), privacy (everything on-device), no subscription, scriptability, quality. Propose bets that would make creators and press say "this is the editor that does X". Also say which catch-up gaps are disqualifying anyway (table stakes that must exist before any differentiation matters).`,
  },
  {
    key: 'leverage',
    brief: `ANGLE: LEVERAGE AND FEASIBILITY. Given the architecture (Rust decides; AVFoundation/Metal does; one-pass compositor; Vision, Core ML, Speech, Sound Analysis, Translation, ScreenCaptureKit available on macOS 26; cloud AI only via the user's own key), which bets give the most visible creator value per unit of build effort and risk, and which reuse what already exists (transcripts and word timings, speech spans, the loudness cache, the action lane, Zoom to Region, the graphics engine, the recorder, the MCP tools)? Name the concrete Apple API or existing module each bet would stand on. Say which of the spec's non-goals (multicam, plugins, motion blur, stabilisation, optical-flow retiming, auto-reframe) are now cheap enough to lift and which should stay out.`,
  },
